From d5380a4456ebee0c5596b35c1b4cf0974a646c24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: onlyfish <linotfish@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 20:09:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] GameMode: add a user maintained list of game packages

Automatic game detection only looks at ApplicationInfo.category, so
sideloaded games, emulators and anything that never declared
android:appCategory are treated as regular apps. That kills the game
power hint, the game mode APIs and anything built on top of them.

Add a user maintained list, stored in Settings.Secure and exposed through
GameModeController:

  game_mode_packages          games the user picked
  game_mode_suppress_heads_up subset that should not pop up while playing
  game_mode_mute              subset that should stay silent while playing

GameManagerService.isPackageGame() and GameClassifierImpl consult that
list first and also accept the legacy FLAG_IS_GAME. The lists are comma
separated, so they stay editable from adb:

  settings put secure game_mode_packages "com.foo,com.bar"

Games are now also offered the system PERFORMANCE and BATTERY modes even
when they never declared a game-mode-config. The bitfield no longer hides
those two behind the manifest opt-in, which only ever described whether
the game handles the mode itself, and battery falls back to a 0.7x render
scaling. Games that did opt in are still left alone.

Notifications honor the two flags: NotificationManagerService skips sound
and vibration, SystemUI suppresses the heads-up. Both need the foreground
package, and asking ActivityTaskManagerInternal for it while the
notification lock is held would take the window manager global lock
there, so a TaskStackListener caches it instead and the read becomes a
volatile field access. Only fullscreen tasks count, split screen and
freeform keep the normal behaviour.

Finally, a per game ANGLE selection writes the existing
angle_gl_driver_selection_pkgs/_values lists that GraphicsEnvironment
reads when a process starts.

